  <title>Braille output</title>
  <p>
    Braille displays communicate with Orca through BRLTTY, a service that connects braille
    displays to applications. Depending on your display and system setup, you may need to
    configure your display in BRLTTY. You may also need to start BRLTTY before running Orca if
    your system does not start it automatically. Orca does not start BRLTTY itself.
  </p>
  <p>
    Orca uses the following indicators to show the state of controls in braille:
  </p>
  <list>
    <item>
      <p>
        Check boxes: <code>&lt; &gt;</code> for unchecked, <code>&lt;x&gt;</code> for checked, and <code>&lt;-&gt;</code> for partially checked.
      </p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <p>
        Radio buttons: <code>&amp; y</code> for unselected and <code>&amp;=y</code> for selected.
      </p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <p>
        Switches: <code>&amp; y</code> for off and <code>&amp;=y</code> for on.
      </p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <p>
        Toggle buttons: <code>&amp; y</code> for not pressed and <code>&amp;=y</code> for pressed.
      </p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <p>
        Read-only text: <code>rdonly</code> indicates that the text cannot
        be edited.
      </p>
    </item>
  </list>
  <p>
    Orca can also mark links, selected text, and text attributes by raising dots beneath the
    affected characters, similar to underlining. See "<link xref="features_braille_display_settings">Display settings</link>" to configure these
    indicators, and "<link xref="features_text_attributes">Text attributes</link>" to choose which
    text attributes are marked.
  </p>
  <section id="settings-braille-output">
    <title>Settings</title>
    <p>
      <em style="strong">Preferences location:</em>
      <gui>Braille</gui> category.
    </p>
    <list>
      <item>
        <p>
          <gui>Braille support</gui>: Turns braille
          output on or off. Default: on.
        </p>
      </item>
    </list>
  </section>
  <section id="commands-panning-and-routing">
    <title>Commands</title>
    <p>
      <em style="strong">Preferences location:</em>
      <gui>Commands</gui> category, <gui>Commands</gui> section, <gui>Braille</gui> group.
    </p>
    <p>
      The commands below control the braille reading location and interact with displayed
      content. Standard keyboard bindings can be changed in Commands preferences. Braille
      display bindings depend on the display and BRLTTY configuration and cannot currently be
      rebound in Orca.
    </p>
    <list>
      <item>
        <p>
          <gui>Pan the braille display to the left</gui>
        </p>
        <list>
          <item>
            <p>
              Keyboard: unbound by default.
            </p>
          </item>
          <item>
            <p>
              Braille: Line Left
            </p>
          </item>
        </list>
      </item>
      <item>
        <p>
          <gui>Pan the braille display to the right</gui>
        </p>
        <list>
          <item>
            <p>
              Keyboard: unbound by default.
            </p>
          </item>
          <item>
            <p>
              Braille: Line Right
            </p>
          </item>
        </list>
      </item>
      <item>
        <p>
          <gui>Return to object with keyboard focus</gui>
        </p>
        <list>
          <item>
            <p>
              Braille: Cursor Position
            </p>
          </item>
        </list>
      </item>
      <item>
        <p>
          <gui>Process a cursor routing key</gui>
        </p>
        <list>
          <item>
            <p>
              Braille: Cursor Routing
            </p>
          </item>
        </list>
        <p>
          Moves the caret to the character at the selected braille cell or activates the item at
          that cell, depending on the application's accessibility support. See "<link xref="features_reviewing_objects_mouse_review">Mouse review and clicking</link>" for clicking and
          its limitations on Wayland. If a flash message is displayed, pressing a routing key
          dismisses that message instead.
        </p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <p>
          <gui>Mark the beginning of a text selection</gui>
        </p>
        <list>
          <item>
            <p>
              Braille: Cut Begin
            </p>
          </item>
        </list>
        <p>
          Marks the start of text to copy. Use the command to mark the end of the text to
          complete the selection and copy it to the clipboard.
        </p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <p>
          <gui>Mark the end of a text selection</gui>
        </p>
        <list>
          <item>
            <p>
              Braille: Cut Line
            </p>
          </item>
        </list>
        <p>
          Marks the end of text to copy after its start has been marked. Orca selects the text
          and copies it to the clipboard. Paste it using the destination application's usual
          paste command. See "<link xref="features_clipboard">Clipboard</link>" for clipboard
          requirements on Wayland.
        </p>
      </item>
    </list>
  </section>
